Fix AP EAPOL receive path when MLO is configured but not established.

THE root cause of every client WPA2 4-Way Handshake failing against every AP
VAP, on both the controller and the extender: the AP sends M1, the client
answers M2, the AP never acts on it, retries M1 four times and deauths the
client with reason 15. No client could ever associate to a fronthaul.

recv_data_frame() chose which address to match an EAPOL frame against with:

    interface_mac = (wifi_hal_is_mld_enabled(interface) &&
                     vap_mode == wifi_vap_mode_ap) ?
                    wifi_hal_get_mld_mac_address(interface) : interface->mac;

and silently returned when neither dest nor src matched it. wifi_hal_is_mld_
enabled() reflects static configuration, not runtime state. On this platform
MLO bring-up fails outright:

    setup_mlo_vap:6670: MLD interface is enabled, but interface name is unset
                        - skipping
    platform_pre_create_vap:437: Failed to setup link for MLD ID 0 with VAP idx 0
    platform_pre_create_vap:437: Failed to setup link for MLD ID 0 with VAP idx 1

so the MLD address is unusable while clients keep addressing the VAP's own
MAC. Every M2 was therefore discarded before it reached hostapd, which never
sent M3.

Match the VAP's own MAC, and additionally the MLD MAC once one actually
exists. This is the same "enabled vs actually established" distinction the
hapd->mld guards elsewhere in this HAL already make (see patch 0005), and it
keeps the receive path correct under partial MLO bring-up rather than trusting
configuration state.

Localised with kernel kprobes (host 6.8, BTF offsets) after the userspace
explanations were exhausted -- the frames were provably fine all the way to
the HAL:
  * ieee80211_deliver_skb() fires once per M2 on wifi1
  * packet_rcv() receives them: 8 of 8, et=0x8e88, dst=the VAP MAC
    02:00:00:85:11:f6, src=the client 02:00:00:00:0d:00
  * the packet_type servicing them is owned by sk=0xffff8b6096ab0800, which is
    the HAL's own socket on ifindex 11 per /proc/net/packet, and
    sock_def_readable() fired 8 times for that exact sk -- so the BPF filter
    ACCEPTED and the HAL was woken
  * recv_data_frame() then dropped them at the comparison above

Note the drop precedes the existing "version:%d type:%d length:%d" debug
print, so that print is not a measure of function entry -- it counts frames
that survived the MAC check. The mesh backhaul STA was unaffected throughout
because the MLD branch is guarded by vap_mode == wifi_vap_mode_ap.

Not gated behind HWSIM_RADIO: nothing here is hwsim-specific. Any platform
whose MLO setup fails after config enables it hits the same silent drop.

--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
@@ -3133,18 +3133,41 @@ void recv_data_frame(wifi_interface_info_t *interface)
     }
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_GENERIC_MLO) && defined(CONFIG_IEEE80211BE)
-    interface_mac = (wifi_hal_is_mld_enabled(interface) &&
-                     (interface->vap_info.vap_mode == wifi_vap_mode_ap)) ?
-                    wifi_hal_get_mld_mac_address(interface) : interface->mac;
-    if (memcmp(eth_hdr->dest, interface_mac, sizeof(mac_address_t)) == 0) {
+    /* Accept a frame addressed to (or sent from) the VAP's own MAC, and also
+     * the MLD MAC once one has actually been established. Keying this off
+     * wifi_hal_is_mld_enabled() alone is wrong: it reflects static
+     * configuration, not runtime state. When MLO bring-up fails -- "MLD
+     * interface is enabled, but interface name is unset - skipping" -- the MLD
+     * address is unusable, while clients still address the VAP's own MAC, so
+     * every EAPOL frame was silently dropped here. Same "enabled vs actually
+     * established" distinction the hapd->mld guards elsewhere make. */
+    interface_mac = NULL;
+    if (wifi_hal_is_mld_enabled(interface) &&
+        (interface->vap_info.vap_mode == wifi_vap_mode_ap)) {
+        interface_mac = wifi_hal_get_mld_mac_address(interface);
+        if ((interface_mac != NULL) && is_zero_ether_addr(interface_mac)) {
+            interface_mac = NULL;
+        }
+    }
+    if ((memcmp(eth_hdr->dest, interface->mac, sizeof(mac_address_t)) == 0) ||
+        ((interface_mac != NULL) &&
+         (memcmp(eth_hdr->dest, interface_mac, sizeof(mac_address_t)) == 0))) {
         // received frame
         is_rx_frame = true;
         memcpy(sta, eth_hdr->src, sizeof(mac_address_t));
-    } else if (memcmp(eth_hdr->src, interface_mac, sizeof(mac_address_t)) == 0) {
+    } else if ((memcmp(eth_hdr->src, interface->mac, sizeof(mac_address_t)) == 0) ||
+               ((interface_mac != NULL) &&
+                (memcmp(eth_hdr->src, interface_mac, sizeof(mac_address_t)) == 0))) {
         // transmitted frame
         memcpy(sta, eth_hdr->dest, sizeof(mac_address_t));
     } else {
-        // drop
+        wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: %s dropping eapol, dst %02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x "
+            "src %02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x matches neither vap mac nor mld mac\n",
+            __func__, __LINE__, interface->name,
+            eth_hdr->dest[0], eth_hdr->dest[1], eth_hdr->dest[2],
+            eth_hdr->dest[3], eth_hdr->dest[4], eth_hdr->dest[5],
+            eth_hdr->src[0], eth_hdr->src[1], eth_hdr->src[2],
+            eth_hdr->src[3], eth_hdr->src[4], eth_hdr->src[5]);
         return;
     }
 #else
